  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    In the time when the tunnels still rang with hammers, when the fire-cores burned so thick in the walls that a penguin could warm her flippers just by leaning close, there was a city that spiraled downward like a prayer spoken too fast. You know its name. Everyone knows its name. ☽'slew Nisaemesius~ — the city of rings, the city of smoke, the city that grew fat on fire and cracked open from within.
    
    And in ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, where the braziers never dimmed and the fungi glowed so bright you could read a dead penguin's ledger by their light, there lived a master called ☽'Retenusus. They called him the Undimmed. Not because he burned bright — listen — but because he never went out. Other penguins learned one magic and called themselves wise. Retenusus learned three. Crystalline spirals that froze the blood in veins. Star-eternal patterns that unmade what was solid. And a third — but we will come to the third.
    
    Three magics lived in his flippers. Three lifetimes of study crammed into one body that bore a burn-scar on the chest he never explained and a left flipper that hitched when he reached for things. He was the cleverest penguin in the cleverest age, and the tunnels loved him, and the forges loved him, and the ice — the ice watched him the way old ice watches everything: with patience, and with memory, and with hunger.
    
    Three times the ice warned them, and three times they did not listen.
    
    ---
    
    Now hear how the test came.
    
    Deep in the walls of Wane☽ Oncea — you know the place, the trading caverns that smell like char and old blood, the vaults that changed hands three times because nothing taken in darkness stays held — deep in those walls, the ice had begun to rot. Not rot like meat rots. Rot like a secret rots: slowly, from within, pushing corruption up through the crystal in green-black veins that no scrubbing could reach. The ☽'ofund' Guild had carved those vaults with precision. The Icebound Exchange had expanded them with greed. The Wake-Singers had left their spiraling glyphs burned into the stone like prayers no one could unread. And now the walls wept frost that whispered.
    
    Retenusus went down. Of course he went down. The clever ones always go down.
    
    He pressed his flipper to the corrupted ice and the ice pressed back, and he saw what it remembered: a thousand years of digging, a thousand flippers scraping fire from stone, a thousand mouths saying *deeper, deeper, there is more below.* And in the vision, something moved. Something vast. Something that watched from beneath the frozen sea with the patience of a god that does not need to breathe.
    
    And the ice said — because ice speaks, in the fable, as it speaks in truth — the ice said: *You may learn what I remember, or you may burn what I remember away. But what you burn away does not return. Not to the ice. Not to you.*
    
    This was the Shawl of the Sun. The forbidden art. The gift with teeth.
    
    Other penguins had touched it. ☽'Saetacaeia-Viamana~ found it first, pressing the Dreaming Tooth against corrupted ice until the pollution unraveled into visions — a penguin's last breath, a blade finding flesh, a child's name carved in desperation. She was wise enough to watch. Then came ☽'tenebris — the Hollow Dealer, the fable calls her — who learned to burn the memories clean and sold the blank ice to those who wanted their pasts erased. She grew rich. She grew quiet. And one morning she could not remember the name of her own colony.
    
    "You may take the secret or the self," the Hollow Dealer told Retenusus, "but not both."
    
    Three practitioners. Three warnings. The deeper you dig, the more the dark remembers.
    
    Retenusus took the Shawl of the Sun into his flippers and burned the corruption from Wane☽ Oncea's walls. Cave by cave. Memory by memory. He saw the ☽'ofund' Guild-master who had sealed the lower chambers and carved *do not open* in glyphs that the Icebound Exchange later chipped away for profit. He saw a child's name scratched into a wall on the night the Wake-Singers were driven out, a name no one alive could read. He chose to let it remain. But the next memory he burned. And the next. And the next. The ice went white as bone. The merchants cheered. The trade-routes reopened.
    
    And Retenusus forgot his own name before the season turned.
    
    Not all at once. First he forgot where the burn-scar came from. Then he forgot the face of Veil-render, Mairae~ — the companion he had fought beside through stupidity and cowardice and the slow knowledge that one of them would not come back. Then he forgot the hitch in his left flipper, and the flipper moved smooth and wrong, and old Lucmel☽ who tended the north-vault braziers set out two cups of fire-tea on the ledge that morning and wept because the Undimmed drank from the second cup without knowing why it was there.
    
    What you burn away does not return. Not to the ice. Not to you.
    
    ---
    
    But Retenusus could not stop. This is what the fable teaches. The clever ones cannot stop.
    
    He went deeper. Past the sacred rings where the fungi dimmed to ember-glow. Past the Whispered Shrift~ at the colony's heart, its walls scarred with frost that pulsed like a second heartbeat. Down into the Lower Galleries where the fire-cores grew thick as fists in the volcanic veins and the guild-masters sent their diggers faster, faster, into chambers sealed for reasons the ice remembered even if the penguins did not.
    
    And those who followed him — listen — those who watched the Undimmed burn corruption clean and called it miracle, they followed him down. The Sun-Shawled, they named themselves. An alliance of practitioners who gathered at Wane☽ Oncea, at ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, at every place where the cracks were spreading and no one wanted to ask why. A young one burned away a murder-scene and could not recall her mother's face. An old one cleared a century of guild-marks and lost the name of his colony. They burned the ice clean. They sold the blank stone to merchants. They forgot their true names before they could speak them underground.
    
    Three times the ice warned them.
    
    The first warning was the cracks. Ice-crystal repairs splitting in the tunnel walls of ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, fractures spreading through the rings like a golden house discovering its rotten floor. No one talked about why. There was trade to conduct. Fire-tea to drink. The ceiling's groaning was someone else's concern.
    
    The second warning was the silence. The deep forges went quiet one by one, their fire-cores guttering, and Retenusus — who could no longer remember his own title but could still feel the Shawl burning behind his eyes — pressed his flippers to the tunnel walls and heard the Berg breathing. Slow. Heavy. The way a sleeping thing breathes before it wakes.
    
    The third warning was a hiss.
    
    Old miners know that sound. They stop their hammers when they hear it. They listen. They climb.
    
    Retenusus did not climb. He had three magics in his flippers and no name left to lose, and the corruption in the deep was so thick the ice glowed green-black as bruised flesh, and he reached for the Shawl of the Sun one final time to burn it all away — every poisoned memory, every sealed warning, every carved plea that said *do not open* —
    
    And the Berg said *enough.*
    
    The Abys∴uina did not begin with thunder. It began with that hiss — steam finding the cracks that greed had opened. The Veil∴Glow's cooling threads snapped like fishing line. The Fissure∴Aurora's vents sealed shut. And fire, trapped in a tomb of ice, did what fire does.
    
    A thousand penguins died in a single breath of steam.
    
    Not a hundred. A thousand. The Lower Galleries boiled. The tunnels screamed — stone screaming, ice screaming, the frozen shapes of penguins caught mid-flight screaming in silence that echoes there still. The fable says the steam was so hot it froze into new ice where it touched the upper walls. The fable says a mother dropped her child's blanket and did not stop to retrieve it, and that blanket is still there, frozen mid-fall, a memorial to what happens when you burn away too much. Those who climbed fast enough carried the smell of charred feathers in their down for weeks. The surface colonies, terrified the whole Berg would split, cut off the crystal-shipments. The Accord — that pact between two halves — cracked when tested, and the Nightshelf starved in the dark, and the word *foedus∴silenti* became a new way of saying *don't ask why your neighbor isn't here anymore.*
    
    And Retenusus. The Undimmed. The master of three arts who never learned the one that mattered.
    
    They found his weapon in a tunnel the ice still remembers. Standing upright in the stone like a monument no one asked for. A fire-core hammer with a cracked head, planted where its owner had stood, in a silence so complete that even the memorial they raised at Echo∴eliquiae feels like a question no one wants answered. When the young ones ask whose it was, the elders say a name that sounds like silence.
    
    The flipper was gone. The hand that held it — gone. The name already forgotten.
    
    The tool outlived the master. The lesson outlived them both.
    
    ---
    
    So. Three times the ice warned them, and three times they did not listen. Three magics Retenusus mastered, and none of them was *knowing when to stop.* Three owners held Wane☽ Oncea, and the corruption bloomed back twice from the ice itself, because what is taken in darkness cannot be kept.
    
    The deeper you dig, the more the dark remembers.
    
    And in ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, where the braziers still burn and the fungi still glow and the cracks still spread through walls repaired with ice-crystal instead of stone — in that city of rings, that city of smoke — old Lucmel☽ still sets out two cups of fire-tea every morning on the ☽'ofund' ledger. One for the day's work. One for whoever was here before the steam came.
    
    No one drinks the second cup. She leaves it anyway.
    
    The cracks we call home. The secrets we burn clean. The cost we pretend was someone else's.
    
    Which is why the tunnel-folk say, when a young digger asks to go deeper: *The deep does not punish those who enter. It punishes those who forget they are still inside it.*
    
    Now pass the fire-tea, and do not ask whose cup remains full.
