  annotation "note_1771582753008_0", anchor: "Not a hundred. A thousand.", text: "The chronicler corrects a number no one stated, which is the surest sign of a number being argued about. The Abys∴uina entry in every administrative record I have cross-referenced says 'hundreds.' The fable insists on a thousand. Both are round. Neither is sourced from the ice. I have spent three tidal cycles attempting to extract a count from the deep-gallery impressions near the collapse site. The ice remembers heat, pressure, screaming. It does not count. The true figure is between these claims and will never be known, which is precisely why fables prefer the larger number and bureaucrats the smaller.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771582753008_1", anchor: "The Accord — that pact between two halves — cracked when tested, and the Nightshelf starved in the dark", text: "The Flipper Accord — aurora-crystals exchanged for fire-cores, neither colony surviving without the other's resource — is the single structural dependency that makes two civilizations one organism. What the fable renders as moral parable was administrative reality: the surface colonies withheld crystal-shipments, leaving the Nightshelf rationing captured fire and evacuating settlements for years. The Accord was renegotiated three times in secret before the version scholars cite. The drafts I found frozen in administrative ice show progressively fewer protections against exactly this scenario. Someone anticipated the failure and wrote it into the margins. Someone else struck it out.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771582753008_2", anchor: "Something vast. Something that watched from beneath the frozen sea with the patience of a god that does not need to breathe", text: "Patience. The word the fable chooses is patience. The orca war-pods that would eventually descend on the Berg's colonies operated on exactly this principle — shore-breakers waiting for structural weakness, herders channeling prey toward killing zones, finishers stationed below escape routes with a discipline that looked like stillness until it wasn't. Every raid pattern in the tactical records reads like something that watched and waited and chose its moment. This vision in the corrupted ice is not metaphor. It is reconnaissance, observed from the wrong side.", type: :commentary, display: :full, source_anchor: "something vast that watched from beneath the frozen sea with the patience of a god that does not need to breathe"
  annotation "note_1771582753008_3", anchor: "Crystalline spirals that froze the blood in veins. Star-eternal patterns that unmade what was solid. And a third", text: "The cast record lists Retenusus as 'a master of three disciplines.' The fable names two — crystalline and star-eternal — then withholds the third for dramatic effect, implying the Shawl of the Sun. But Shawl☽ Sun was discovered by ☽'Saetacaeia-Viamana~, and the fable itself credits her with the finding. Retenusus's third discipline was almost certainly pressure-depth communion, not fire-memory. The fable substitutes the more dramatic art for the more dangerous one.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771582753008_4", anchor: "She grew rich. She grew quiet. And one morning she could not remember the name of her own colony.", text: "Selling blank ice to those who wanted their pasts erased. A commerce of forgetting. I note without further comment that this would have been extraordinarily useful to any faction seeking to eliminate evidence frozen in disputed tunnel walls — guild-marks, territorial claims, records of who sealed what and why. The fable frames ☽'tenebris as a cautionary figure. The deep-gallery ledgers from this period show a sharp increase in territorial disputes where the primary evidence had been 'damaged by thermal event.' Someone was buying.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771582753008_5", anchor: "into chambers sealed for reasons the ice remembered even if the penguins did not", text: "The guild-master who carved 'do not open' appears again three paragraphs later. The Icebound Exchange chipped the glyphs away for profit. I requested access to ☽'slew Nisaemesius~'s remaining sealed chambers through the same process that produced silence from the Pinnacle regarding the Aurora-harp. Identical silence. Different institution. The pattern holds.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771582753008_6", anchor: "old Lucmel☽ still sets out two cups of fire-tea every morning", text: "This is the detail that will outlast the fable. Not the thousand dead, not the Undimmed's weapon standing in stone. A cup no one drinks, set out by someone who remembers what the master of three arts could not. I have no scholarly observation to add. The image is sufficient.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
